 {"schema_version":"0.1","evidence_record_id":"ev-glp1-step1-withdrawal-weight-regain-001","extraction_method":"manual_source_audit","extraction_status":"draft_manual_prototype","source_doi":"10.1111/dom.14725","source_title":"Weight regain and cardiometabolic effects after withdrawal of semaglutide: The STEP 1 trial extension","source_type":"paper","study_type":"observational_trial_extension","research_question":"After withdrawal of semaglutide and lifestyle intervention following 68 weeks of treatment, what body-weight change occurred during the 52-week STEP 1 off-treatment extension?","claim_text":"In the exploratory STEP 1 extension, participants previously assigned semaglutide regained a mean 11.6 percentage points of body weight during 52 weeks after semaglutide and lifestyle-intervention withdrawal, compared with 1.9 percentage points after placebo and lifestyle-intervention withdrawal.","evidence_direction":"qualifies","population":"327 adults with overweight or obesity without diabetes who completed 68 weeks of STEP 1 semaglutide or placebo treatment and entered the selected-site off-treatment extension.","intervention":"Withdrawal of once-weekly subcutaneous semaglutide 2.4 mg and lifestyle intervention after week 68.","comparator":"Withdrawal of placebo and lifestyle intervention after week 68.","outcome":"Body-weight regain and net body-weight change during 52 weeks off treatment, from week 68 to week 120.","result_summary":"From week 68 to week 120, the prior semaglutide group regained 11.6 percentage points (SD 7.7) and the prior placebo group regained 1.9 percentage points (SD 4.8). Net mean change from baseline at week 120 was -5.6% (SD 8.9) and -0.1% (SD 5.8), respectively; the authors described the semaglutide group as regaining two-thirds of its prior weight loss.","source_span":{"local_pdf_path":"papers/corpora/glp1_weight_loss/PMC9542252.pdf","page_number":2,"section":"Abstract / Results"},"short_source_excerpt":"Following treatment withdrawal, 