 {"schema_version":"0.1","evidence_record_id":"ev-glp1-gao-meta-analysis-safety-discontinuation-001","extraction_method":"manual_source_audit","extraction_status":"draft_manual_prototype","source_doi":"10.3389/fphar.2022.935823","source_title":"Efficacy and safety of semaglutide on weight loss in obese or overweight patients without diabetes: A systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials","source_type":"paper","study_type":"systematic_review_meta_analysis","research_question":"In randomized trials of adults with overweight or obesity without diabetes, how did semaglutide compare with placebo for adverse events, serious adverse events, discontinuation due to adverse events, and common gastrointestinal events?","claim_text":"The meta-analysis reported higher pooled risks with semaglutide than placebo for adverse events, serious adverse events, discontinuation due to adverse events, nausea, and diarrhea, while hypoglycemia did not differ.","evidence_direction":"qualifies","population":"Adults with overweight or obesity without diabetes in semaglutide randomized controlled trials; six studies (4,512 participants) contributed adverse-event and serious-adverse-event data, and eight studies (4,567 participants) contributed discontinuation-due-to-adverse-event data.","intervention":"Subcutaneous semaglutide across included trial doses and durations.","comparator":"Placebo.","outcome":"Any adverse event, serious adverse event, adverse event leading to discontinuation, nausea, diarrhea, and hypoglycemia.","result_summary":"Compared with placebo, pooled risk ratios were 1.10 (95% CI 1.05 to 1.16) for adverse events, 1.34 (1.10 to 1.65) for serious adverse events, and 2.29 (1.74 to 3.01) for adverse events leading to discontinuation. Nausea RR was 2.58 (2.33 to 2.86) and diarrhea RR was 2.01 (1.79 to 2.27); hypoglycemia did not differ (RR 0.94, 0.66 to 1.34).","source_span":{"local_pdf_path":"papers/corpora/glp1_weight_loss/fphar-2022-935823.pdf","page_number":10,"section":"Results / Adverse events"},"short_source_excerpt":"Combined statistics noted that semaglutide increased the incidences of AEs, SAEs, and DAEs compared with placebo, especially DAEs","limitations":["The any-adverse-event analysis had high heterogeneity (I2=82%), reflecting variation across included studies.","Included trials varied in semaglutide dose, treatment duration, and population details; pooled estimates are not one uniform 2.4 mg regimen or treatment window.","The review included a limited number of studies and divided one study into seven separate trials for analyses, which the authors noted could affect homogeneity and publication bias.","Aggregate event incidence does not preserve event timing, recurrence, severity within each category, or individual benefit-harm tradeoffs.","The review predates later semaglutide trials and cannot provide a complete current class-wide safety assessment."],"uncertainty_notes":"This record qualifies weight-loss efficacy with pooled tolerability and discontinuation findings from the same review. It does not compute a benefit-harm balance, establish causality for every event, or generalize to all GLP-1 receptor agonists.","confidence_note":"High confidence that the extracted pooled estimates match the source; this is not a clinical safety rating or a confidence score for individual treatment decisions.","provenance":{"created_by":"OpenAI Codex independent source audit","created_date":"2026-08-03","source_url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2022.935823/full","pdf_url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/pharmacology/articles/10.3389/fphar.2022.935823/pdf","license_type":"CC BY","license_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","metadata_source":"data/corpora/glp1_weight_loss/sources.csv","secondary_review":{"reviewer":"OpenAI Codex","reviewer_type":"ai_assisted_independent_source_audit","review_date":"2026-08-03","source_basis":"legally usable local p