 {"schema_version":"0.1","evidence_record_id":"ev-glp1-glide-liraglutide-post-lagb-weight-001","extraction_method":"manual_source_audit","extraction_status":"draft_manual_prototype","source_doi":"10.1038/s41366-023-01368-4","source_title":"Laparoscopic gastric banding with liraglutide and type 2 diabetes: a randomised placebo-controlled trial (GLIDE)","source_type":"paper","study_type":"pilot_randomized_controlled_trial","research_question":"In adults with obesity and type 2 diabetes after laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding, did six months of liraglutide 1.8 mg improve body weight compared with placebo?","claim_text":"In the underpowered GLIDE pilot, adding liraglutide 1.8 mg after gastric banding did not improve body weight versus placebo at six months; at 12 months, six months after treatment ended, adjusted body weight was higher in the former liraglutide arm.","evidence_direction":"qualifies","population":"27 adults with obesity and type 2 diabetes randomized after laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding; 13 received liraglutide and 14 received placebo.","intervention":"Subcutaneous liraglutide 1.8 mg once daily for six months after laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding.","comparator":"Placebo for six months after laparoscopic adjustable gastric banding.","outcome":"Between-group body-weight difference at six months and at 12 months, six months after investigational treatment ended; body weight was a secondary outcome.","result_summary":"At six months, adjusted body weight did not differ between groups (difference 2.0 kg, 95% CI -4.2 to 8.1; p=0.50). At 12 months, adjusted body weight was 8.2 kg higher in the former liraglutide arm (95% CI 1.6 to 14.9; p=0.02). The trial randomized 27 participants against a planned sample of 58 and was significantly underpowered.","source_span":{"local_pdf_path":"papers/corpora/glp1_weight_loss/PMC10599987.pdf","page_number":1,"section":"Abstract / Results and Conclusions"},"short_source_excerpt":"There was no difference 